      "page_title": "Functions Are First-Class Objects",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. First-Class, Four Ways",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Assign: `f = greet` — the name is just a label",
            "Store: `[greet, bye]`, `{'csv': parse_csv}`",
            "Pass: `sorted(xs, key=len)`",
            "Return: an outer function hands back an inner one"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "def greet(): return 'hi'",
            "f = greet        # the object itself",
            "f()              # 'hi' — same function",
            "g = greet()      # 'hi' — the VALUE"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Missing Parens Slip",
          "text": "`@name` needs a callable, so bare `@deco` takes no parens — but a decorator *factory* must be called: `@repeat(3)` applies `repeat(3)`'s return value."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Every Function Carries Attributes"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Attribute",
            "Holds"
          ],
